<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kimmeridge</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kimmeridge is a coastal village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, which gives its name to the Kimmeridge Clay of the Jurassic period. The local oil shale was worked from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> into the Roman period, when it was turned on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lathe">lathes</a> into bracelets and carved into furniture. The bay is also known for its fossils and for an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oil-well">oil well</a> that has operated since 1959.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kimmeridge.jpg" alt="Kimmeridge" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Parish · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kimmeridge_main_street_-_geograph.org.uk_-_251704.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kimmeridge lies on the Isle of Purbeck, about 4.5 miles south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wareham">Wareham</a> and 7 miles west of Swanage, on a slope between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> and a curve of hills. Most of the parish lies on Kimmeridge Clay, with Portland stone on Smedmore Hill and Swyre Head. The clay holds bands of bituminous shale and dolomite that form flat ledges in Kimmeridge Bay at low tide. The village gives its name to the Kimmeridgian stage of the Late Jurassic, and the clay is one of the source rocks of the oil and gas of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> basins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> and under Roman rule the black shale, known as blackstone or Kimmeridge coal, was turned on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lathe">lathes</a> into armlets; the waste discs, found at several sites, were taken by 18th-century antiquaries for coins and called coal money. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> also burned the shale to boil sea water for salt. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> the parish held three settlements, Kimmeridge, Little Kimmeridge and Smedmore, of which only Kimmeridge survives as a village of any size.</p><p>Lord Mountjoy tried and failed to make alum here in the mid 16th century. In the first half of the 17th century Sir William Clavell, who moved into his new Smedmore House in 1632, tried salt, then alum, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> merchants holding the sole right from Charles I seized his property and destroyed the works; a port and a glassworks, made with Sir Robert Mansell and Abraham Bigo, failed too, and his pier was wrecked by a storm in 1745. An Act of Parliament of 1847 allowed tramways to carry shale to Weymouth for oil, varnish and dyes, though its sulphurous smell limited its use. In 1959 an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oil-well">oil well</a> was sunk above the bay; it produced 200,000 tonnes between 1961 and 1974.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kimmeridge's coast is part of the Jurassic Coast World Heritage Site, designated in 2001, and the parish lies within the Dorset National Landscape. The parish church was mostly rebuilt in 1872 but keeps parts from the 12th century, and the Clavell Tower above Hen Cliff was moved back from the eroding cliff edge. The Museum of Jurassic Marine Life, which opened in October 2016, shows Steve Etches's collection of more than 2,000 fossils.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kimmeridge” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Durotriges buried their dead in crouched inhumations, unlike the cremation rites of the south-east, and <b>Kimmeridge</b> shale was worked into bracelets.</blockquote><a href="/state/durotriges">Durotriges</a></li></ul></section>
